('banana','banana',NULL,'/bəˈnænə/','common',4.0,'plain','["noun", "adjective"]','An elongated curved tropical fruit of a banana plant, which grows in bunches and has a creamy flesh and a smooth skin.','noun','Borrowed from Portuguese banana or Spanish banana, derived from a Niger-Congo language spoken in the Guinea region. Specific derivation is unclear. Possible ancestor or cognate languages include Wolof banaana, Eastern Maninkakan banana, and Vai ꕒꘌꕯ (ɓaana) or ꕒꕌꕯ (ɓaana), possibly from Arabic بَنَان (banān, “fingertip, banana”). However, Ay Baati Wolof (Munro & Gaye, 1997) posits that Wolof banaana is itself derived from Portuguese banana.
The racial slur derives from the notion that they are “Yellow (East-Asian) on the outside, but White (Westernized) on the inside”.',574,'200plus',1,0.8,74),
('First Contact Day','First Contact Day',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["noun"]','April 5th, an annual occasion to celebrate Star Trek.','noun','first contact + day.
From the fictional instance of official first contact in the Star Trek fictional universe for Earth, occurring in the 1996 film First Contact on 5 April 2063. Taken by Trekkies as a day to celebrate Star Trek.',230,'200plus',0,0.55,75),
('enmity','enmity',NULL,'/ˈɛn.mɪ.ti/','common',3.2,'plain','["noun"]','The quality of being an enemy; a hostile or unfriendly disposition.','noun','From Middle English enemyte, from Old French enemisté, ennemistié, from Late Latin, Vulgar Latin *inimīcitās, *inimīcitātem, from Latin inimīcus (“enemy”). Cognate with French inimitié, Portuguese inimizade, Spanish enemistad. By surface analysis, en(e)m(y) + -ity.',265,'200plus',1,0.8,76),
('Texas-sized','Texas-sized',NULL,NULL,'rare',NULL,'capitalized,hyphenated','["adjective"]','Very large for its type.','adjective','Texas is the second-largest state of the United States by both area and population.',83,'80_120',0,0.5,77),
('Lisan ud-Dawat','Lisan ud-Dawat',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized,hyphenated','["proper noun"]','A Neo-Indo-Aryan language derived from Gujarati spoken by Isma''ili Shi''a communities in Gujarat. It uses the Arabic script.','proper noun','From Gujarati لسان دعوۃ البهرة (Lisan al-Dawat).',48,'40_80',1,0.4,78),
('Airy-0','Airy-0',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized,hyphenated,digits','["proper noun"]','A crater on Mars, located within the larger Airy Crater, used as the determining landmark for the Martian prime meridian.','proper noun','From Airy Crater, the larger crater this smaller crater is located within, and 0° of the prime meridian. Named after George Biddell Airy, a British astronomer who helped establish the Greenwich Meridian as the Prime Meridian on Earth.',234,'200plus',0,0.59,79),
('nebris','nebris',NULL,'/ˈnɛbɹɪs/','rare',0.63,'plain','["noun"]','The skin of a fawn, as worn by Dionysus, and as worn in his honor by his male followers and female followers (maenads) and other votaries in Ancient Greece. It has also been shown as a panther or fox skin in different contexts.','noun','From Latin nebris, from Ancient Greek νεβρίς (nebrís), earlier νεβρός (nebrós, “fawn”).',87,'80_120',1,0.6,80),
('aborticide','aborticide',NULL,'/əˈbɔɹ.tɪˌsaɪd/','obscure',-0.08,'plain','["noun"]','The act of destroying a fetus in the womb; feticide.','noun','From abortion + -cide (“killing; killer”).',42,'40_80',1,0.4,81),
('stromatolite','stromatolite',NULL,NULL,'uncommon',2.32,'plain','["noun"]','A laminated, columnar, rock-like structure constituting a large share of all fossils from 3.5 to 0.5 billion years ago, with some still being formed at present, some or all of which result from the deposit of minerals by microorganisms such as cyanobacteria.','noun','From Ancient Greek στρώματα (strṓmata) (plural of στρῶμα (strôma, “layer”)) + λίθος (líthos, “stone, rock”).',108,'80_120',1,0.6,82),
('trip to the woodshed','trip to the woodshed',NULL,NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','An occasion on which a reprimand or punishment is administered privately.','noun','Probably derived from the previous term "horseshedding," meaning to take a person privately (such as to a horse shed or woodshed, separate buildings away from the house) for private cajoling or reprimand.',204,'200plus',1,0.6,83),
('Gaeta','Gaeta',NULL,NULL,'uncommon',2.36,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A coastal town in the province of Latina, Lazio, Italy.','proper noun','Borrowed from Italian Gaeta.',28,'lt40',1,0.44,84),
('consecrate','consecrate',1,'/ˈkɑnsəkɹeɪt/','common',3.41,'plain','["verb"]','To declare something holy, or make it holy by some procedure.','verb','First attested in the late 14ᵗʰ century, in Middle English; inherited from Middle English consecraten (“to dedicate, consecrate (an altar, church); to ordain (a bishop), anoint (a king, a pope); to devote one to religious life”), from consecrat(e) (“consecrated”, used as the past participle of consecraten) + -en (verb-forming suffix), borrowed from Latin cōnsecrātus, perfect passive participle of cōnsecrāre, see -ate (verb-forming suffix).',443,'200plus',1,0.8,85),
('umpteen','umpteen',NULL,'/ˈʌmp.tiːn/','uncommon',2.14,'plain','["determiner"]','Relatively large but unspecified in number.','determiner','From ump (“a colloquial name for the dash "—" in Morse code”) + -teen. Unrelated to ump as in umpire, and simply a mnemonic rhyming aid.',136,'120_200',1,0.65,86),
('omiai','omiai',NULL,NULL,'scarce',1.15,'plain','["noun"]','A Japanese custom in which unattached individuals are introduced to each other to consider the possibility of marriage.','noun','Borrowed from Japanese お見合い (おみあい, o-miai).',43,'40_80',1,0.49,87),
('Delft','Delft',NULL,'/ˈdɛlft/','uncommon',2.72,'capitalized','["noun", "proper noun"]','Blue and white earthenware produced in Delft, Netherlands.','noun','Borrowed from Dutch Delft. Piecewise doublet of delved.',55,'40_80',1,0.58,88),
('Danco Island','Danco Island',NULL,NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["proper noun"]','An island of Antarctica.','proper noun','From French Danco + English island. Partial calque of French île Danco. Named after Belgian polar explorer Émile Danco.',119,'80_120',1,0.64,89),
('oenomancy','oenomancy',NULL,NULL,'obscure',-0.64,'plain','["noun"]','divination by studying wine','noun','From Ancient Greek οἶνος (oînos, “wine”) in conjunction with μαντεία (manteía, “divination”). By surface analysis, oeno- + -mancy.',130,'120_200',1,0.65,90),
('snorkel parka','snorkel parka',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','a parka whose hood encircles and protrudes forward from the face','noun','The shape of the hood resembles the portion of a snorkel visible above the water',80,'80_120',0,0.45,91),
('Ido','Ido',NULL,'/ˈi.doʊ/','uncommon',2.54,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A constructed language; a reform of Esperanto and Idiom Neutral.','proper noun','Borrowing from Ido Ido. Began as a pseudonym, probably directly from Esperanto ido (“offspring”).',97,'80_120',1,0.64,92),
('discubitory','discubitory',NULL,NULL,'marginal',-1.33,'plain','["adjective"]','leaning; fitted for a reclining posture','adjective','From Latin discumbere, discubitum (“to lie down, recline at table”), from dis- + cumbere (“(in comparative) to lie down”).',122,'120_200',1,0.6,93),
('Myanmar','Myanmar',NULL,'/ˈmjɑnˌmɑɹ/','common',3.81,'capitalized','["adjective", "proper noun"]','Of or relating to Myanmar, Burmese.','adjective','Borrowed, initially into British English, from Burmese မြန်မာ (mranma), the formal Burmese name of the main ethnic group in Myanmar; ဗမာ (ba.ma), from which Burma is derived, is the informal name. Both Burmese terms are also adjectives meaning "Burmese" (related to Myanmar/Burma, its, people, language, etc). Like the r in the word Burma, the r at the end of Myanmar was not originally pronounced (and is not present in the Burmese term), it is a vowel-lengthening device used by non-rhotic dialects; compare gorm.',515,'200plus',1,0.8,94),
('kekomi','kekomi',NULL,NULL,'rare',0.14,'plain','["noun"]','A thrusting kick.','noun','Borrowed from Japanese 蹴込み (kekomi)',35,'lt40',1,0.39,95),
('stiff-necked','stiff-necked',NULL,NULL,'rare',NULL,'hyphenated','["adjective"]','Having a stiff neck; suffering from torticollis','adjective','From stiff + neck + -ed.
(figurative): Calque of Hebrew קשה ערף (Exodus 33). A biblical term similar to Swedish styvnackad, Ancient Greek σκληροτράχηλος (sklērotrákhēlos), Russian жестоковыйный (žestokovyjnyj), German halsstarrig etc.',234,'200plus',1,0.8,96),
('Peragine','Peragine',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Italian.','proper noun','Borrowed from Italian Peragine.',31,'lt40',1,0.34,97),
('big back','big back',NULL,'/ˈbɪɡˌbæk/','rare',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','An obese person, or someone with gluttonous eating habits.','noun','The term initially emerged as British slang, referring specifically to an individual, often a woman, characterized by prominent buttocks. Subsequently, this usage was adopted into African-American Vernacular English (AAVE), gaining widespread recognition through Sir Mix-a-Lot''s song Baby Got Back (1992). However, the term''s connotation shifted to a more derogatory sense following a reference by radio personality Charlamagne Tha God to ReesaTeesa''s highly viral series Who TF Did I Marry? on the social media platform TikTok. In this context, he described the behavior depicted in the series as big back behavior.',616,'200plus',1,0.7,98),
('orexigenic','orexigenic',NULL,NULL,'scarce',1.41,'plain','["noun", "adjective"]','Appetite stimulant','noun','From Ancient Greek ὄρεξις (órexis, “appetite”) + -genic.',56,'40_80',1,0.45,99),
('possible','possible',NULL,'/ˈpɑ.sə.bəl/','common',5.4,'plain','["noun", "adjective"]','Able but not certain to happen; neither inevitable nor impossible.','adjective','From Middle English possible, from Old French possible, from Latin possibilis (“possible”), from posse, possum (“to be able”); see power. Displaced Middle English acumendlic (“possible”), from Old English ācumendlīċ (“possible”). Compare also Old English mihtelīċ (“strong, capable, powerful, possible”), which was cognate with Old High German mahtlīh (“possible”) and Old Norse máttulígr (“mighty, possible”). Compare also Dutch mogelijk (“possible”) and German möglich (“possible”).',484,'200plus',1,0.8,100);
