('scarlet-collar','scarlet-collar',NULL,NULL,'rare',NULL,'hyphenated','["adjective"]','Of or pertaining to female entrepreneurs in the Internet sex industry.','adjective','From the color traditionally associated with adultery, combined with the word collar in imitation of similar constructs such as blue-collar and white-collar.',157,'120_200',0,0.6,101),
('Egorovca','Egorovca',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A commune and village in Fălești Raion, Moldova.','proper noun','Unadapted borrowing from Romanian Egorovca.',43,'40_80',1,0.44,102),
('undesired','undesired',NULL,NULL,'uncommon',2.91,'plain','["adjective"]','Not desired; unwanted.','adjective','From Middle English undesired; equivalent to un- + desired.',59,'40_80',1,0.5,103),
('Robling','Robling',NULL,NULL,'scarce',1.04,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from German.','proper noun','Borrowed from German Röbling.',29,'lt40',1,0.39,104),
('amblygnathous','amblygnathous',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'plain','["adjective"]','Having relatively short and blunt jaws.','adjective','From Ancient Greek ἀμβλύς (amblús, “blunt”) + -gnathous. By surface analysis, ambly- + -gnathous.',97,'80_120',1,0.5,105),
('homophyly','homophyly',NULL,NULL,'obscure',-0.07,'plain','["noun"]','That form of homology due to common ancestry (phylogenetic homology), in opposition to homomorphy, to which genealogic basis is wanting.','noun','homo- + Ancient Greek φυλή (phulḗ, “clan”) + -y.',48,'40_80',1,0.45,106),
('namus','namus',NULL,NULL,'scarce',1.24,'plain','["noun"]','A concept of virtue and honor within a family, typically relating to chastity of female family members.','noun','From Arabic نَامُوس (nāmūs). Doublet of nome and nomos.',55,'40_80',1,0.49,107),
('Chungking','Chungking',NULL,NULL,'uncommon',2.05,'capitalized,pointer_only','["proper noun"]','Dated form of Chongqing.','proper noun','From the Jesuit romanization of the Nanking court dialect Mandarin 重慶 /重庆 (Chóngqìng), from before the modern palatalization of [kʰ] to [t͡ɕʰ], reinforced by Postal Romanization.',178,'120_200',1,0.7,108),
('quasi','quasi',NULL,'/ˈkwɑːˌzaɪ/','common',4.0,'plain','["adjective"]','Resembling or having a likeness to the named thing.','adjective','Learned borrowing from Latin quasi (“as if”).',45,'40_80',1,0.54,109),
('spagyric','spagyric',NULL,'/spəˈd͡ʒɪɹɪk/','rare',0.87,'plain','["noun", "adjective"]','Pertaining to alchemy; alchemical, especially regarding medicine.','adjective','From Late Latin spagyricus, from Ancient Greek σπάω (spáō, “I draw, pull”) + ἀγείρω (ageírō, “I assemble”).',107,'80_120',1,0.6,110),
('galleon','galleon',NULL,'/ˈɡæ.li.ən/','uncommon',2.66,'plain','["noun"]','A large, three-masted, square rigged sailing ship with at least two decks.','noun','From Middle French galion or Spanish galeón. Originally an augmentative form of a Romance language cognate of galley, the word spread around the Mediterranean from the 12th century, taking on different meanings depending on place and time.',239,'200plus',1,0.8,111),
('Xunyang','Xunyang',NULL,NULL,'rare',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A county-level city of Ankang, Shaanxi, China, formerly a county.','proper noun','From the Hanyu Pinyin romanization of the Mandarin 旬陽 /旬阳.',58,'40_80',1,0.45,112),
('cadrans','cadrans',NULL,NULL,'obscure',-0.07,'plain','["noun"]','An instrument by which a gem is adjusted while being cut.','noun','From French cadran (“quadrant”).',32,'lt40',1,0.35,113),
('bric-a-brac','bric-a-brac',NULL,'/ˈbɹɪkəˌbɹæk/','rare',NULL,'hyphenated','["noun"]','Small ornaments and other miscellaneous display items of little value.','noun','Borrowed from French bric-à-brac (“miscellaneous items of little value”), apparently from à bricq et à bracq (“at random; haphazardly”); bricq and bracq are expressive onomatopoeias of obscure origin.',200,'200plus',1,0.69,114),
('seethe','seethe',NULL,'/sið/','common',3.19,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','A state of boiling or frothing; ebullition, seething; hence, extreme heat; much activity.','noun','The verb is derived from Middle English sethen, seeth (“to boil, seethe; to cook; etc.”) [and other forms], from Old English sēoþan (“to boil, seethe; to cook; etc.”), from Proto-West Germanic *seuþan, from Proto-Germanic *seuþaną (“to boil, seethe”), from Proto-Indo-European *h₂sewt-, *h₂sew-, *h₂sut- (“to move about, roil, seethe”).
The noun is derived from the verb.
Cognates
* Danish syde (“to seethe, boil”)
* Dutch zieden (“to boil, seethe”)
* German sieden (“to boil, seethe”)
* Gothic 𐍃𐌰𐌿𐌸𐍃 (sauþs, “burnt offering, sacrifice”)
* Icelandic sjóða (“to boil, seethe”)
* Low German seden (“to seethe”)
* Norwegian Bokmål syde (“to boil, seethe”)
* Norwegian Nynorsk sjoda, syda (“to boil, seethe”)
* Scots seth, seith (“to seethe”)
* Swedish sjuda (“to boil, seethe”)
* West Frisian siede (“to boil”)',807,'200plus',1,0.8,115),
('hetaera','hetaera',NULL,'/hɪˈtɪəɹə/','scarce',1.47,'plain','["noun"]','A mistress.','noun','From Ancient Greek ἑταίρᾱ (hetaírā), feminine of ἑταῖρος (hetaîros, “companion”).',81,'80_120',1,0.55,116),
('nonpareil','nonpareil',NULL,'/ˌnɑnpəˈɹɛl/','uncommon',2.18,'plain','["noun", "adjective"]','A person or thing that has no equal; a paragon.','noun','From Late Middle English non-parail (“unparalleled, nonpareil”) [and other forms], from Middle French nonpareille, nonpareil (“unparalleled”) (obsolete), from non- (prefix meaning ‘not’) + pareil (“alike, like, same”). Pareil is derived from Old French pareil, from Late Latin pāriculus (“equal; like; of a number: even”), from Latin pār (“equal; like; of a number: even; suitable”) + -culus (a variant of -ulus (suffix forming diminutives)).
Noun sense 4 (size of type standardized at 6-point) is usually taken to derive from the attractive type cut by the brothers Giovanni and Gregorio De Gregori (fl. 1482–1503 and 1496–1527 respectively) for their 1498 edition of the divine offices in Venice; it was for a long time the smallest-sized type in use.',753,'200plus',1,0.8,117),
('later','later',NULL,'/ˈleɪ.tɚ/','common',5.49,'plain','["adjective", "adverb", "interjection"]','Coming afterward in time (used with than when comparing with another time).','adjective','* Adverb: From Middle English later, latere, from Old English lator, equivalent to late + -er.
* Adjective: From Middle English later, latere, from Old English lætra, equivalent to late + -er.
Cognate with Saterland Frisian leeter (“later”), West Frisian letter (“later”), Dutch later (“later”), German Low German later (“later”).',330,'200plus',1,0.8,118),
('Caehopkin','Caehopkin',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A village in Tawe-Uchaf community, Powys, Wales (OS grid ref SN8212).','proper noun','From (or partly from) Welsh.',28,'lt40',1,0.3,119),
('watchet','watchet',NULL,'/ˈwæt͡ʃɪt/','scarce',1.43,'plain','["noun", "adjective"]','A light blue colour.','noun','From Middle English wachet, waget; compare French vaciet (“whortleberry”), Latin vaccīnium (“blueberry”).',105,'80_120',1,0.65,120),
('e:598c8b7fa2b0e3ef3007a0e77fd079a48f4e6f78b76a0142b25c1dfe74ca1ec2','bill',2,'/ˈbɪl/','common',5.15,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','The beak of a bird, especially when small or flattish; sometimes also used with reference to a platypus, turtle, or other animal.','noun','From Middle English bill, bil, bille, bile, from Old English bile (“beak (of a bird); trunk (of an elephant)”), of unknown origin. Perhaps from a special use of Old English bil, bill (“hook; sword”) (see below).',211,'200plus',1,0.75,121),
('footless','footless',NULL,NULL,'scarce',1.61,'plain','["adjective"]','Without feet.','adjective','From Middle English fotles, from Old English *fōtlēas (only found as fēþelēas (“footless”)), from Proto-Germanic *fōtalausaz (“footless”), equivalent to foot + -less. Cognate with Dutch voetloos, German fußlos, Swedish fotlös, Icelandic fótalaus (“footless, legless”).',268,'200plus',1,0.8,122),
('rockaway','rockaway',NULL,NULL,'uncommon',2.63,'plain','["noun"]','A light, low, four-wheeled carriage with standing top, open at the sides, but with waterproof curtains for bad weather.','noun','Probably from Rockaway Beach, where they were used. From the Unami lekawe (“sandy place”) or a Munsee cognate of that word.',123,'120_200',1,0.69,123),
('Haifeng','Haifeng',NULL,NULL,'scarce',1.15,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A county of Shanwei, Guangdong, China.','proper noun','From Mandarin 海豐／海丰 (Hǎifēng).',30,'lt40',1,0.35,124),
('bateau','bateau',NULL,'/bætˈəʊ/','uncommon',2.19,'plain','["noun"]','A small, flat-bottomed type of boat.','noun','Borrowed from French bateau.',28,'lt40',1,0.44,125),
('Soohoo','Soohoo',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Taishanese.','proper noun','From Taishanese 司徒 (lhu¹ hu³). Compare Tagalog Soho.',52,'40_80',1,0.4,126),
('porcelain','porcelain',NULL,'/ˈpɔɹs(ə)lɪn/','common',3.56,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','A hard white translucent ceramic, originally made by firing kaolin, quartz, and feldspar at high temperatures but now also inclusive of similar artificial materials; also often (figurative) such a material as a symbol of the fragility, elegance, etc. traditionally associated with porcelain goods.','noun','Borrowed from Middle French porcelaine (“cowrie, wampum; china, chinaware”), from Old Italian porcellana (“cowrie; china, chinaware”), from Italian porcello (“piglet”). The material was so called because of its resemblance to the shell of the cowrie. Why the cowrie was named with a word meaning “piglet” is unclear.',316,'200plus',1,0.8,127),
('Aretas','Aretas',NULL,NULL,'scarce',1.33,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','The name of various kings of the Nabataeans','proper noun','Borrowed from Ancient Greek Ἀρέτας (Arétas) or Latin Aretas.',60,'40_80',1,0.54,128),
('balachong','balachong',NULL,NULL,'obscure',-0.64,'plain','["noun"]','A Malaysian and Singaporean form of ngapi, a Southeast Asian condiment made from fermented and compressed fish or shrimp.','noun','From Malay belacan.',19,'lt40',1,0.35,129),
('typhlosole','typhlosole',NULL,NULL,'rare',0.82,'plain','["noun"]','A fold in the surface of the intestines of some invertebrates etc that increases its digestive surface area.','noun','From typhl- (“blind, caecum”) + Ancient Greek σωλήν (sōlḗn, “channel, pipe”).',77,'40_80',1,0.45,130),
('doney','doney',2,NULL,'scarce',1.8,'plain','["noun"]','girl, sweetheart, darling, young woman, woman','noun','Perhaps from Catalan dona or Spanish doña; perhaps introduced to England by sailors who had visited Spanish or Italian portshttp://www.mudcat.org/thread.cfm?threadid=54922&messages=35',183,'120_200',1,0.75,131),
('Kin Sha Kiang','Kin Sha Kiang',NULL,NULL,'marginal',NULL,'multiword,capitalized,pointer_only','["proper noun"]','Alternative form of Jinsha Jiang.','proper noun','From the Nanjing-dialect (later Postal Romanization) romanization of 金沙江 (Jīnshājiāng).',87,'80_120',0,0.45,132),
('Männlein','Männlein',NULL,'/ˈmænlaɪn/','rare',NULL,'capitalized,nonascii','["noun"]','A little man; a dwarf, a mannikin.','noun','Borrowed from German Männlein (“a little man”), from Mann (“man”) + -lein (diminutive suffix); compare English manling.',119,'80_120',1,0.64,133),
('coruscant','coruscant',NULL,'/kəˈɹʌskənt/','uncommon',2.22,'plain','["adjective"]','Emitting flashes of light; glittering.','adjective','From Latin coruscāns (“glittering”), present participle of coruscō.',67,'40_80',1,0.5,134),
('Nordmøre','Nordmøre',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized,nonascii','["proper noun"]','A district of Møre og Romsdal, Norway.','proper noun','Borrowed from Norwegian Nordmøre.',33,'lt40',1,0.34,135),
('azadi','azadi',NULL,NULL,'uncommon',2.25,'plain','["noun"]','Freedom, independence.','noun','Borrowed from Hindustani آزادی (āzādī) / आज़ादी (āzādī), from Classical Persian آزادی (āzādī).',94,'80_120',1,0.64,136),
('Hatague','Hatague',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Spanish.','proper noun','From Spanish Hatague (“son of Hatagueno”), from Hatagueno, variant of Crisostomo (“Crisostome”), of Spanish origin.',115,'80_120',1,0.5,137),
('Spenserian stanza','Spenserian stanza',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["noun"]','A strophe of eight decasyllabic lines and an alexandrine, having three rhymes: the first and third; the second, fourth, fifth, and seventh; and the sixth, eighth, and ninth (ABABBCBCC).','noun','Invented by English poet Edmund Spenser (1552/53–1599) for his epic poem The Faerie Queene (1590–96).',101,'80_120',1,0.5,138),
('abarognosis','abarognosis',NULL,'/ˌæb.əɹ.əɡˈnoʊ.sɪs/','obscure',-0.55,'plain','["noun"]','A loss of the ability to realise or estimate the weight of an object.','noun','From Ancient Greek ἀ- (a-, “not”) + βάρος (báros, “weight”) + γνῶσις (gnôsis, “knowledge”).',91,'80_120',1,0.55,139),
('nanny','nanny',NULL,'/ˈnæni/','common',3.59,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','A child''s nurse.','noun','From nan (“grandmother; nursemaid”) + -y. The root is from nana (“grandma; nanny”), which is from nanna (“grandmother”), which is possibly derived from Proto-Celtic *nana (“grandmother”). See also Proto-Brythonic *nanī, Welsh nain (“grandmother”), Galician nana (“mama”), Spanish nana (“granny; nanny; mommy; housekeeper”), Sicilian nanna (“grandma”), Italian nonna (“granny”), Late Latin nonna (“nun; tutoress; old woman”), Norman nonne (“nun”), Old French nonain (“nun”). All probably ultimately from a Proto-Indo-European root imitative of a child babbling, similar to Ancient Greek νάννα (nánna).
(1795) It has often been assumed that the English term was originally a widespread child''s word for "female adult other than mother" (compare Greek νάννα (nánna, “aunt”), nanna).
On the other hand, according to recent research of the Dutch historical linguists Hans Beelen and Nicoline van der Sijs (published in Onze Taal, September 2018), on which see also etymologiebank.nl, in Dutch), the term nanny (and the British colloquial nan for "grandmother") may actually be eponymous, viz. being originally an affective form (i.e. a hypocoristic) of the popular female name Anne. The Dutch statesman and scientist Constantijn Huygens Jr. made the following observation during one of his many sojourns in England (noted in his Journaal, dated 13 December 1692): "Yesterday I received 10lb of chocolate again, from niece Becker, and she had Nanny, her maid, bringing me the money that she had owed me" (Gisteren kreegh 10 ℔ choccolate wederom van nicht Becker, en had Nanny, haer meidt, geweest om mij ''t geldt, dat van haer hebben most, te brengen). Beelen and van der Sijs therefore assumed that "since many female domestic servants were named "Nan" or "Nanny", the name became a sobriquet for the profession of "maid, childminder" in the 18ᵗʰ century". ("Omdat veel vrouwelijke huisbedienden in het Engels de voornaam Nan of Nanny hadden, verschoof de betekenis in de achttiende eeuw naar die van een beroepsaanduiding: ‘meid, kindermeisje’")',2041,'200plus',1,0.8,140),
