('wreath','wreath',1,'/ɹiθ/','common',3.37,'plain','["noun"]','Something twisted, intertwined, or curled.','noun','From Middle English wreth, wrethe (“coiled or rounded shape; decorative garland, wreath; chaplet, crown; ring”, noun), from Old English *wreoþa, wriþa (“band, coil, ring”), from Proto-West Germanic *wriþō, from Proto-Germanic *wriþô, from *wrīþaną (“to twist; to weave”), from Proto-Indo-European *wreyt- (“to twist”). Doublet of wreathe and writhe.',349,'200plus',1,0.8,214),
