('locule','locule',NULL,NULL,'uncommon',2.08,'plain','["noun"]','A little hollow; a loculus.','noun','Compare French locule. See loculus.',35,'lt40',1,0.4,301),
('evil-speaking','evil-speaking',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'hyphenated','["noun"]','The act of speaking ill or maliciously of something or someone; blasphemy, slander; gossip.','noun','From Middle English *ivel-spekynge (suggested by Middle English yvelspeker, yvel speker, ivel spekare (“evil-speaker”)), equivalent to evil (noun) + speaking (noun).',165,'120_200',1,0.6,302),
('lamblia','lamblia',NULL,NULL,'uncommon',2.13,'plain','["noun"]','Giardia lamblia, an intestinal parasite that causes giardiasis.','noun','From New Latin, Lambl + -ia, named after Austrian physician Vilém Dušan Lambl (1824–1895).',90,'80_120',1,0.64,303),
('mammothrept','mammothrept',NULL,'/ˈmaməθɹɛpt/','obscure',-0.6,'plain','["noun"]','A spoiled child.','noun','From Late Latin mammothreptus (“kept at the breast too long”), from Koine Greek μαμμόθρεπτος (mammóthreptos, “brought up by one’s grandmother”), from Ancient Greek μάμμη (mámmē, “grandmother”) + θρεπτός (threptós) verbal adjective from τρέφω (tréphō, “to bring up”).',266,'200plus',1,0.8,304),
('fuckity fuck','fuckity fuck',NULL,NULL,'marginal',NULL,'multiword','["adjective", "interjection"]','Used to express dismay, discontent or frustration.','interjection','Variant of fuckity with reduplication of fuck. Compare yackety-yak for the formation.',85,'80_120',0,0.45,305),
('Grande Ile','Grande Ile',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["proper noun"]','An island of Strasbourg, Bas-Rhin department, Alsace, Grand Est, France, on the Ill River.','proper noun','From French Grande Île, Grande-Île (literally “grand island, big island”).',74,'40_80',1,0.44,306),
('Limon','Limon',NULL,NULL,'uncommon',2.36,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Spanish.','proper noun','Borrowed from Spanish Limón. Doublet of Lemon.',46,'40_80',1,0.58,307),
('materially','materially',NULL,NULL,'common',3.7,'plain','["adverb"]','In a material manner; with regard to physical things or characteristics.','adverb','From Middle English materialy; equivalent to material + -ly.',60,'40_80',1,0.5,308),
('Clough','Clough',NULL,'/klʌf/','uncommon',2.88,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname transferred from the common noun.','proper noun','* As an English surname, from the noun clough.
* As a Welsh surname, from cloff (“lame”), from Late Latin cloppus, perhaps ultimately imitative of a limping person.
* As an Irish place name, from Irish an Chloch, "the stone".',225,'200plus',1,0.8,309),
('surmount','surmount',NULL,'/sɚˈmaʊnt/','common',3.19,'plain','["verb"]','To get or be over without touching or resting on; to overcome.','verb','From Middle English surmounten, from Old French surmonter (“to rise above, surmount”), from sur- (“above”) + monter (“to mount”), equivalent to sur- + mount.',157,'120_200',1,0.75,310),
('Ante','Ante',NULL,NULL,'common',3.33,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname.','proper noun','Various origins:
* As a Spanish and Catalan surname, perhaps from ante (“elk; suede”).
* As a Dutch and North German surname, from Ente (“duck”).',145,'120_200',1,0.8,311),
('complaisant','complaisant',NULL,'/kəmˈpleɪsənt/','scarce',1.98,'plain','["adjective"]','Compliant.','adjective','From French complaisant (“willing to please”), from complaire, from Latin complacēre (“please well”), from com- (“with”) + placeō (“please”).',141,'120_200',1,0.7,312),
('facetious','facetious',NULL,'/fəˈsiːʃəs/','uncommon',2.51,'plain','["adjective"]','Treating serious issues with (often deliberately) inappropriate humour; flippant.','adjective','From French facétieux, from Latin facētia (“jest, wit, humor”), from facētus (“witty, jocose, facetious”).',106,'80_120',1,0.65,313),
('uh-huh','uh-huh',NULL,'/ʌˈhʌ/','rare',NULL,'hyphenated','["particle"]','Yes; yeah.','particle','Imitative of non-verbal sounds used to indicate agreement or to allow the speaker to continue talking. Compare synonymous mhm.',126,'120_200',0,0.6,314),
('zombied-out','zombied-out',NULL,NULL,'marginal',NULL,'hyphenated','["adjective"]','Like a zombie in being sluggish, numb, listless, and vacant.','adjective','First attested in 1976; zombie + -ed + out; compare the later verb zombie out (1981).',85,'80_120',0,0.45,315),
