('commandery','commandery',NULL,'/kəˈmænd(ə)ɹi/','uncommon',2.19,'plain','["noun"]','A chapter of a secret order, especially the Knights Templar (order affiliated with Freemasonry).','noun','From French commanderie, from Late Latin commendaria, from Latin commendare.
In the Chinese administrative division sense, used as a translation of 府 (fǔ) and 郡 (jùn).',167,'120_200',1,0.8,337),
('Kiringul','Kiringul',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A cave in North Korea, said to have been the home of the mythical kirin.','proper noun','From Korean 기린굴 (girin''gul, literally “Kirin''s Grotto”).',56,'40_80',1,0.44,338),
('boba tea','boba tea',NULL,'/ˌboʊbə ˈti/','rare',NULL,'multiword,pointer_only','["noun"]','Synonym of bubble tea.','noun','From boba + tea, related to English ball.',41,'40_80',1,0.45,339),
('neoprene','neoprene',NULL,'/ˈniɵpɹin/','uncommon',2.55,'plain','["noun"]','A synthetic rubber, a polymer of chloroprene, commonly used in wetsuits, laptop sleeves, orthopedic braces, electrical insulation, liquid and sheet-applied elastomeric membranes and flashings, car fan belts, etc.','noun','US, 1930s; genericized trademark for DuPont brand of polychloroprene. neo- (“new”) + isoprene (dropping the iso-), the latter from terpene. Originally called duprene (blend of DuPont and isoprene); the “new” is because neoprene was the first mass-produced general-purpose synthetic rubber.',289,'200plus',1,0.74,340),
('fuzzy navel','fuzzy navel',NULL,NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','A cocktail, usually made with peach schnapps and orange juice.','noun','There are several theories about when and where the drink was invented, but it gets its name from the fuzz of the peach and the navel orange used to make it.',157,'120_200',0,0.6,341),
('scold','scold',NULL,'/ˈskoʊ̯ld/','common',3.47,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','A person who habitually scolds, in particular a troublesome and angry woman.','noun','The noun is from Middle English scold(e), skald(e), first attested in the 12th or 13th century (as scold, scolde, skolde, skald). The verb is from Middle English scolden, first attested in the late 1300s. Most dictionaries derive the verb from the noun and say the noun is probably from Old Norse skald (“poet”) (cognate with Icelandic skáld (“poet, scop”)), as skalds sometimes wrote insulting poems, though another view is that the Norse and English words are cognate to each other and to Old High German skeltan (whence Modern German schelten (“to scold, chide”)), Old Dutch skeldan (whence Modern Dutch schelden (“to scold, berate”)), all inherited from Proto-Germanic *skeldaną (“scold”).',693,'200plus',1,0.8,342),
('joyfully','joyfully',NULL,'/ˈd͡ʒɔɪfəli/','uncommon',2.96,'plain','["adverb"]','In a joyful manner; joyously.','adverb','From Middle English joifulliche, joyfully, equivalent to joyful + -ly.',70,'40_80',1,0.5,343),
('herefor','herefor',NULL,'/hɪəˈfɔː/','scarce',1.02,'plain','["adverb"]','For this: instead or in consideration of this, with a view to this.','adverb','From the Middle English hēr-for; equivalent to here + for; compare herefore, therefor, and wherefor, as well as the Danish herfor, the Dutch hiervoor, the German hierfür, and the Swedish härför.',194,'120_200',1,0.8,344),
('Schmidlin','Schmidlin',NULL,NULL,'scarce',1.07,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from German.','proper noun','Borrowed from German Schmidlin. This surname is mostly found in Switzerland.',76,'40_80',1,0.49,345),
('kalsilite','kalsilite',NULL,NULL,'rare',0.93,'plain','["noun"]','a rare mineral, a form of KAlSiO₄, found in volcanic rocks in parts of Uganda','noun','From the chemical symbols for potassium (K), aluminium (Al) and silicon (Si), and -lite.',88,'80_120',0,0.5,346),
('Torne Valley Finnish','Torne Valley Finnish',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["proper noun"]','Meänkieli (language spoken by some 40,000–70,000 people in Northern Sweden)','proper noun','After Torne, the name of the river that separates Finland and Sweden in the north; compare Swedish tornedalsfinska and Finnish tornionlaaksonsuomi.',147,'120_200',1,0.65,347),
('mockingbird','mockingbird',NULL,'/ˈmɑkɪŋˌbɝd/','uncommon',2.98,'plain','["noun"]','Any of various long-tailed American songbirds of the genera Mimus and Melanotis, noted for their ability to mimic calls of other birds.','noun','From mocking + bird, from the ability of the birds to mimic sounds and, in some cases, hum as a wake-up call.',109,'80_120',0,0.55,348),
('quequisque','quequisque',NULL,NULL,'obscure',-0.91,'plain','["noun"]','new cocoyam','noun','From Spanish quequisque.',24,'lt40',1,0.35,349),
('treasure trove','treasure trove',NULL,NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','A hidden treasure, subsequently discovered.','noun','From Anglo-Norman tresor trové (“found treasure”), calque of Latin thesaurus inventus, where the past participle trové was interpreted in English as a noun.
Perhaps related to trophy.',183,'120_200',1,0.75,350),
('Basilides','Basilides',NULL,NULL,'scarce',1.32,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A male given name of historical usage','proper noun','Borrowed from Latin Basilidēs, from Ancient Greek Βασιλείδης (Basileídēs).',74,'40_80',1,0.54,351),
('Chisasibi','Chisasibi',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','an Indian reserve and village municipality in the Nord-du-Québec region of Quebec, Canada.','proper noun','From Cree ᒋᓴᓯᐱ (cisasipi, “great river”).',41,'40_80',1,0.35,352),
('e:f00a5a403398eb34c38b37799c274027f949c72c59c9f0f201757709ada35d99','Eloi',2,'/ˈiːlɔɪ/','uncommon',2.1,'capitalized','["noun"]','A group of people who have been domesticated or intentionally dumbed down.','noun','Coined by British science fiction author H. G. Wells in 1895 as a fictional species in The Time Machine.',104,'80_120',0,0.59,353),
('cooking with gas','cooking with gas',NULL,NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword','["phrase"]','Functioning particularly effectively; achieving something substantial.','phrase','To cook with natural gas fuel has been occurring since at least the 19th century when the petroleum industry came into being and new products and markets were developed.
The idiomatic phrase entered the popular lexicon as part of an advertising slogan in late 1930s or early 1940s, for American Gas Association. The slogan "Now you''re cooking with gas!" was coined by Deke Houlgate, an employee of AGA, who worked with Bob Hope to insert the phrase into his comedy routines as subtle product placement. The initial idea was to compete with the increasing popularity of electric stoves in the 1930s. However by 1940 it came to have a broader idiomatic usage and figurative meaning because of the way it was used by Bob Hope, Bing Crosby, Jack Benny and even a Daffy Duck cartoon, eclipsing its original intent as advertising. The slogan was repeated throughout the 1941-1942 radio season by many radio stars.
Also used by jazz musicians to praise a performance.',960,'200plus',1,0.69,354),
('haplo-','haplo-',NULL,'/ˈhæplə-/','scarce',1.08,'hyphenated','["prefix"]','Haploid: having a single set of unpaired chromosomes.','prefix','Combining form from Ancient Greek ἁπλόος (haplóos, “single, simple”).',69,'40_80',1,0.45,355),
('wawe','wawe',NULL,NULL,'scarce',1.13,'pointer_only','["noun"]','Alternative form of waw (“wave”).','noun','From Middle English wawe, waghe. Not the same word as wave.',59,'40_80',1,0.45,356),
('raffish','raffish',NULL,'/ˈɹæfɪʃ/','scarce',1.84,'plain','["adjective"]','Characterized by careless unconventionality; rakish.','adjective','From late 18th century raff (“persons among the lowest class in society”) + -ish, still retained in contemporary English with riffraff. From Old French raffer (“to wear away”), of Germanic origin. Compare German raffen. Compare rip (“to tear”), rap (“to snatch”).',263,'200plus',1,0.8,357),
('prickmadam','prickmadam',NULL,NULL,'marginal',-1.31,'plain','["noun"]','Any of several species of stonecrop, such as Petrosedum rupestre, formerly used as ingredients of vermifuge medicines.','noun','From French trique-madame. Compare Dutch tripmadam.',51,'40_80',1,0.45,358),
('Clinton News Network','Clinton News Network',NULL,NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["proper noun"]','CNN.','proper noun','Blend of Clinton + Cable News Network. In reference to allegations that the network''s coverage is biased in favor of Bill and Hillary Clinton. Although the term was initially coined during Bill Clinton''s presidency, it later experienced a surge in popularity during Hillary Clinton''s presidential campaign in 2016.',314,'200plus',0,0.69,359),
('medrogestone','medrogestone',NULL,NULL,'rare',0.24,'plain','["noun"]','A progestin.','noun','From me(thyl)[Term?] + gest- (“steroid, progestogen”) + -one.',61,'40_80',1,0.4,360),
('Cyfarthfa','Cyfarthfa',NULL,NULL,'scarce',1.1,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A community and ward in Merthyr Tydfil borough county borough, Wales.','proper noun','From Welsh.',11,'lt40',1,0.35,361),
('e:d239932bbc29fc991977dab4e05d28104aa8f560380a227f4d27d70fa16c7ec7','kish',2,'/kɪʃ/','uncommon',2.5,'plain','["noun"]','The graphite formed as a byproduct in iron smelting.','noun','Compare German Kies (“gravel, pyrites”).',40,'40_80',1,0.5,362),
('cybernetics','cybernetics',NULL,'/ˌsaɪ.bə(ɹ)ˈnɛ.tɪks/','common',3.0,'plain','["noun"]','The theory/science of communication and control in living organisms or machines.','noun','From Ancient Greek κυβερνήτης (kubernḗtēs, “steersman”), from κυβερνάω (kubernáō, “to steer, drive, guide, act as a pilot”) (whence English govern). The term is attested since at least 1948 in the book Cybernetics by Norbert Wiener, influenced by the cognate term and doublet governor, the name of an early control device proposed by James Clerk Maxwell in 1868. Note also the 1830s French cybernétique (“the art of governing”). Also doublet of Kubernetes.',456,'200plus',1,0.8,363),
('bakhoor','bakhoor',NULL,NULL,'obscure',-0.83,'plain','["noun"]','incense in the form of scented chips or blocks, used in Arab countries','noun','Borrowed from Arabic بَخُور (baḵūr).',36,'lt40',1,0.39,364),
('res ipsa loquitur','res ipsa loquitur',NULL,NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword','["phrase"]','The import of a thing or situation is obvious.','phrase','Unadapted borrowing from Latin rēs ipsa loquitur (“the thing speaks for itself”, literally “the thing itself speaks”).',118,'80_120',1,0.63,365),
('transmute','transmute',NULL,'/tɹænzˈmjuːt/','uncommon',2.89,'plain','["verb"]','To change or convert one thing to another, or from one state or form to another.','verb','From Latin trānsmūtāre, from trans + mūtāre.',44,'40_80',1,0.5,366),
('Leichenhaus','Leichenhaus',NULL,NULL,'rare',NULL,'capitalized','["noun"]','A heated space with an attendant, where dead bodies were kept until they showed signs of decomposition, ensuring that a live person would not be buried; a waiting mortuary.','noun','Borrowed from German Leichenhaus, from Leiche (“corpse”) + -n- + Haus (“house”).',80,'80_120',1,0.59,367),
('Zhuyin fuhao','Zhuyin fuhao',NULL,'/t͡ʃuːˌɪn fuːˈhaʊ/','rare',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["proper noun"]','A phonetic alphabet used to transcribe Mandarin for students of that language and for inputting Chinese, now used primarily only in Taiwan. Variants also exist for other Chinese lects spoken in Taiwan.','proper noun','From the Hanyu Pinyin romanization of the Mandarin 注音符號 /注音符号 (zhùyīn fúhào, literally “symbol(s) for annotation of sounds”).',125,'120_200',1,0.69,368),
('Waunarlwydd','Waunarlwydd',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A village and community in the City and County of Swansea, Wales (OS grid ref SS6095).','proper noun','From Welsh.',11,'lt40',1,0.3,369),
