('decamp','decamp',NULL,'/diːˈkæmp/','uncommon',2.52,'plain','["verb"]','To break camp and move on.','verb','* French décamper, Old French descamper.',40,'40_80',1,0.55,371),
('still waters run deep','still waters run deep',NULL,NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword','["proverb"]','A person with a calm appearance has, or may have, considerable inner emotion, character, or intellect.','proverb','Of Latin origin, but possibly popularized by William Shakespeare, c. 1590, in "Henry VI," part 2, act 3, scene 1:
: Smooth runs the water where the brook is deep;
: And in his simple show he harbours treason.',208,'200plus',1,0.65,372),
('winds of change','winds of change',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','The inexorable process of inevitable societal and political change and progress over time.','noun','Coined by UK Prime Minister Harold Macmillan in 1960 in a speech advocating decolonization made in Cape Town, South Africa, which referred to the "wind of change […] blowing through this continent", later shifting to the plural "winds of change" in popular usage.',263,'200plus',1,0.64,373),
('Lung-shan','Lung-shan',NULL,NULL,'marginal',NULL,'capitalized,hyphenated,pointer_only','["proper noun"]','Alternative form of Longshan.','proper noun','From the Wade–Giles romanization of Mandarin 龍山 /龙山 (Lóngshān) (Lóngshān, lit. "Dragon Mountain") Wade-Giles romanization: Lung²-shan¹.',135,'120_200',1,0.6,374),
('TANSTAAFL','TANSTAAFL',NULL,NULL,'rare',NULL,'capitalized,pointer_only','["phrase"]','Acronym of there ain''t no such thing as a free lunch: something advertised as being "free" will invariably have hidden costs.','phrase','Acronym, originating in the early 20th century, and popularized by Robert Heinlein''s novel The Moon Is a Harsh Mistress.',120,'120_200',0,0.6,375),
('Upper Bauko','Upper Bauko',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["noun"]','A group of barangays in Bauko, Mountain Province, Philippines.','noun','From upper + Bauko, referring to its altitude and not its cardinal location (it is located at the south of Bauko).',114,'80_120',0,0.45,376),
('nanophanerophyte','nanophanerophyte',NULL,'/ˌnænoʊˈfænəɹəˌfaɪt/','rare',0.23,'plain','["noun"]','Any phanerophyte between about 10 and 80 inches in height','noun','From nano- (“tiny”) + phanerophyte, from Ancient Greek φανερός (phanerós, “visible”) + -phyte (“plant”).',104,'80_120',1,0.55,377),
