('Wyton','Wyton',NULL,NULL,'scarce',1.22,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A village in Houghton and Wyton parish, Huntingdonshire district, Cambridgeshire, England (OS grid ref TL2772).','proper noun','From Old English wīċ (“village”) + tūn (“enclosure; settlement, town”).',71,'40_80',1,0.45,385),
('skully','skully',1,NULL,'scarce',1.76,'plain','["noun"]','A street game in which players attempt to flick caps into a series of squares drawn on the ground.','noun','From skull + -y; (game): from the skulls which are drawn around the center square in the game.',94,'80_120',0,0.5,386),
('Grove cell','Grove cell',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["noun"]','An early electric primary cell consisting of a zinc anode in dilute sulfuric acid and a platinum cathode in concentrated nitric acid, the two separated by a porous ceramic pot.','noun','Named after its inventor, Welsh physical scientist William Robert Grove.',72,'40_80',1,0.44,387),
('mallet','mallet',NULL,'/ˈmælɪt/','common',3.03,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','A type of hammer with a larger-than-usual head made of wood, rubber or similar non-iron material, used by woodworkers for driving a tool, such as a chisel. A kind of maul.','noun','Inherited from Middle English maylet, borrowed from Old French maillet, from mail + -et.',88,'80_120',1,0.69,388),
('lifeway','lifeway',NULL,NULL,'uncommon',2.33,'plain','["noun"]','One''s path through life; a lifestyle or behaviour, now especially when seen as traditional.','noun','From Middle English *lif-wei (attested as lifes wege, lives wegas), from Old English līfweġ. By surface analysis, life + way.',125,'120_200',1,0.75,389),
('dolomite','dolomite',NULL,'/ˈdoʊ.ləˌmaɪt/','common',3.17,'plain','["noun"]','An evaporite consisting of a mixed calcium and magnesium carbonate, with the chemical formula CaMg(CO₃)₂; it also exists as the rock dolostone.','noun','From French dolomite, coined March 1792 by Nicolas de Saussure, named after French mineralogist and engineer Déodat de Dolomieu (1750–1801) who described the stone in 1791 by adding -ite, from place name Dolomieu, Isère.',220,'200plus',1,0.78,390),
('praline','praline',NULL,'/ˈpɹeɪˌlin/','uncommon',2.14,'plain','["noun"]','A confection made from almonds and other nuts, caramelized sugar, and sometimes milk or cream.','noun','Borrowed from French praline.',29,'lt40',1,0.44,391),
('mete','mete',1,'/miːt/','common',3.07,'plain','["verb"]','To dispense, measure in order to dispense, allot (especially punishment, reward etc.).','verb','From Middle English meten, from Old English metan (“to measure, mete out, mark off, compare, estimate; pass over, traverse”), from Proto-West Germanic *metan, from Proto-Germanic *metaną (“to measure”), from Proto-Indo-European *med- (“to measure, consider”).
Cognate with Scots mete (“to measure”), Saterland Frisian meete (“to measure”), West Frisian mjitte (“to measure”), Dutch meten (“to measure”), German messen (“to measure”), Swedish mäta (“to measure”), Latin modus (“limit, measure, target”), Ancient Greek μεδίμνος (medímnos, “measure, bushel”), Ancient Greek μέδεσθαι (médesthai, “care for”), Old Armenian միտ (mit, “mind”).',636,'200plus',1,0.8,392),
('vinous','vinous',NULL,'/ˈvaɪnəs/','scarce',1.88,'plain','["adjective"]','Pertaining to or having the characteristics of wine.','adjective','From Late Middle English vinous, vinose (“consisting of, containing, or made of wine”), from Latin vīnōsus (“fond of wine; wine-flavoured”), from vīnum (“wine”) + -ōsus (adjective-forming suffix meaning ‘full of, prone to’).',224,'200plus',1,0.7,393),
('salonnier','salonnier',NULL,NULL,'rare',0.11,'plain','["noun"]','A salon host.','noun','From French salonnier.',22,'lt40',1,0.35,394),
('customs','customs',1,'/ˈkʌs.təmz/','common',4.42,'plain','["noun"]','The duties or taxes imposed on imported or exported goods.','noun','Plural of custom. Perhaps influenced by or taken as a corruption of customhouse, from Middle English custome hous.',114,'80_120',1,0.6,395),
('spurry','spurry',NULL,NULL,'rare',0.7,'plain','["noun"]','Any of several European annual herbs of the genus Spergula.','noun','Dutch or Old French spurrie; compare German Spergel, New Latin spergula.',72,'40_80',1,0.6,396),
('kathakali','kathakali',NULL,'/ˌkætəˈkæli/','scarce',1.92,'plain','["noun"]','a spectacular lyric dance drama of southern India performed with acrobatic energy and highly stylized pantomime.','noun','Borrowed from Malayalam കഥകളി (kathakaḷi).',42,'40_80',1,0.49,397),
('Hill tetrahedron','Hill tetrahedron',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["noun"]','Any of a certain family of space-filling tetrahedra that are scissor-congruent to a cube.','noun','Discovered in 1896 by M. J. M. Hill, professor of mathematics at the University College London.',95,'80_120',0,0.45,398),
('blemish','blemish',NULL,'/ˈblɛmɪʃ/','common',3.09,'plain','["noun", "verb"]','A small flaw which spoils the appearance of something, a stain, a spot.','noun','From Middle English blemisshen, blemissen, from Old French blemiss-, stem of Old French blemir, blesmir (“make pale, injure, wound, bruise”) (French blêmir), from Old Frankish *blesmijan, *blasmijan (“to make pale”), from Old Frankish *blasmī (“pale”), from Proto-Germanic *blasaz (“white, pale”), from Proto-Indo-European *bʰel- (“to shine”). Cognate with Dutch bles (“white spot”), German blass (“pale”), Old English āblered (“bare, uncovered, bald, shaven”).',461,'200plus',1,0.8,399),
('tercine','tercine',NULL,NULL,'obscure',-1.0,'plain','["noun"]','A cellular layer derived from the nucleus of an ovule and surrounding the embryo sac.','noun','Borrowed from French tercine, from Latin tertius (“the third”).',63,'40_80',1,0.54,400);
