('Backrooms','Backrooms',NULL,'/ˈbækˌɹums/','scarce',1.54,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A fictional environment, originating from an online urban legend and creepypasta, consisting of a seemingly endless maze of empty hallways, often described as being inhabited by monsters, or having multiple levels or floors.','proper noun','From backrooms. Coined by an anonymous poster on the 4chan /x/ board on May 12th, 2019 (see quotation below).',109,'80_120',0,0.54,461),
('noust','noust',NULL,NULL,'rare',0.12,'plain','["noun"]','A trench or other hollow area, sometimes with walls, where a boat can be hauled up and left ashore.','noun','From the (unattested) Norn reflex of Old Norse naust (“boathouse”). Cognate with Faroese neyst, Norwegian naust, Danish nøst (“boat-shed”).',139,'120_200',1,0.8,462),
('metanoete','metanoete',NULL,'/ˌmɛtənəʊˈiːti/','marginal',NULL,'plain','["noun"]','Metanoia.','noun','Borrowed from Ancient Greek μετανοῆτε (metanoête), subjunctive of μετανοῶ (metanoô, “to repent”), from the Gospel of Luke:
:
οὐχί, λέγω ὑμῖν, ἀλλ’ ἐὰν μὴ μετανοῆτε, πάντες ὁμοίως ἀπολεῖσθε.
oukhí, légō humîn, all’ eàn mḕ metanoête, pántes homoíōs apoleîsthe.
I tell you, no! But unless you repent, you too will all perish. ―Luke 13:3 and 13:5, NIV',347,'200plus',1,0.69,463),
('Benin','Benin',NULL,'/bəˈnin/','common',3.03,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','People''s Republic of Benin, from 1975 to 1990','proper noun','Borrowed from French Bénin, from Portuguese Benim, from Itsekiri Ubinu. The term is first documented in the late 1490s upon Portuguese contact with the Itsekiri Kingdom. The etymology is unclear; it appears Benin was always used as an exonym (probably by the Itsekiri and Yoruba) for the Benin Kingdom, which was always referred to as Ẹ̀dó, and older Ìgòdòmigòdò. The leading hypothesis is that the term originally derived from Yoruba ilẹ̀ ìbínú (“land of anger”), which emerges from a folk myth regarding purported founder of the Benin empire, Oranmiyan, who was a Yoruba prince, who referred to the region by this phrase because of his frustration in ruling the land.',669,'200plus',1,0.8,464),
('dog-eared','dog-eared',NULL,'/ˈdɔɡˌɪ(ə)ɹd/','rare',NULL,'hyphenated','["adjective"]','Of a page in a book or other publication: having its corner folded down, either due to having been read many times, or intentionally as a sort of bookmark.','adjective','From dog + eared (“having ears (of a specified type)”), modelled after dog’s-ear (obsolete), due to the similarity of their appearance to the folded ears of certain dogs. The word is analysable as dog-ear (“to fold the corner of a book’s page”) + -ed (suffix forming possessional adjectives) (dog-ear is attested in print later than dog-eared).',344,'200plus',1,0.65,465),
('Jerezano','Jerezano',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from Spanish.','proper noun','Borrowed from Spanish Jerezano. This surname is most commonly found in Honduras.',80,'80_120',1,0.54,466),
