('technical tap','technical tap',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword','["noun"]','A tap or a slap on a device in a way to either make it work correctly again, or break it completely. For example, hitting a TV to get its reception back, or hitting a jukebox back to life.','noun','The phrase became popular in the Second Industrial Revolution, when sometimes vacuum tubes or soldered connections would become loose.',134,'120_200',0,0.55,486),
('Broad Peak','Broad Peak',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["proper noun"]','A mountain of the Gasherbrum, Karakoram; on the Pakistan-China border between Baltistan, Gilgit-Baltistan and Xinjiang. The 12th highest mountain in the world, an eight-thousander.','proper noun','broad + peak. From having a broad appearance. Calque of German Breithorn, from breit (“broad”)+Horn (“peak, prominence, summit”). Named after Breithorn, the mountain in the Swiss Alps. Coined by British explorer Martin Conway in 1892.',234,'200plus',1,0.68,487),
('actus','actus',NULL,'/ˈæktəs/','uncommon',2.72,'plain','["noun"]','A former Roman unit of length, equal to 120 Roman feet (about 35.5 m)','noun','From Latin āctus (“a cattle drive; a cattle path; units of length and area”). Doublet of act.',93,'80_120',1,0.64,488),
('capisce','capisce',NULL,'/kəˈpiʃ/','scarce',1.29,'plain','["verb", "interjection"]','To understand (someone or something).','verb','Unadapted borrowing from the spoken Neapolitan and Sicilian equivalents of either of the following:
* Italian capisce (literally “he, she, etc., understands”), the third-person singular present indicative form; or
* capisci (literally “you understand”) (possibly with the final vowel dropped or reduced in informal speech), the second-person singular present indicative form;
of capire (“to understand”), from Latin capere, the present active infinitive of capiō (“to capture, catch, seize; to comprehend, understand; etc.”), ultimately from Proto-Indo-European *kap- (“to grab, seize; to hold”).',596,'200plus',1,0.8,489),
('boomshakalaka','boomshakalaka',NULL,'/ˈbuːmˌʃɑkəˌlɑkə/','obscure',-0.94,'plain','["noun", "interjection"]','The sound of a slam dunk and the board or chain rattling that follows.','noun','Uncertain, but likely from the filler syllables boom-laka-laka-laka in the lyrics to the song “I Want to Take You Higher” (1969) by Sly and the Family Stone, which appeared as the corruption boom-shaka-laka-laka in subsequent covers; the modern shortened form boomshakalaka, as an apparent reference to “I Want to Take You Higher”, appeared in the military comedy film Stripes (1981). From there, a scriptwriter for the video game NBA Jam (1993) adopted it as onomatopoeic for a slam dunk in lines voiced by Tim Kitzrow, popularizing its use.',542,'200plus',1,0.65,490),
('Krenzer','Krenzer',NULL,NULL,'unattested',NULL,'capitalized','["proper noun"]','A surname from German.','proper noun','Borrowed from German Krenzer.',29,'lt40',1,0.34,491),
('Landslide Lyndon','Landslide Lyndon',NULL,NULL,'rare',NULL,'multiword,capitalized','["proper noun"]','Lyndon B. Johnson.','proper noun','Sarcastic nickname from the fact that Lyndon B. Johnson''s 1948 run to a be a Texas senator needed a runoff in order to be successful, which LBJ won by 87 votes over Coke Stevenson, in a state with a large population. After 1964, there were more sincere uses due to his 1964 United States presidential election landslide win over Barry Goldwater.',345,'200plus',0,0.65,492),
